### Recover and identify blur completion (2/2)

**Severity:** `high`

CreateBlurredCopy currently runs before the try/finally, so an exception leaves this screen marked busy and prevents its queued screenshot from advancing. Move creation into the guarded operation, retain the failed operation in telemetry, reset/advance only its original state, and dispose results whose state has been replaced.

```suggestion
    private void RunBlur(ScreenInfo screenInfo, ScreenshotBlurState expectedState, Bitmap image)
    {
        Bitmap? blurredImage = null;
        try
        {
            using (ScreenshotBlurPipeline.CurrentTelemetry.BeginTimer(new { }, "CreateBlurredCopy"))
            using (image)
            {
                blurredImage = BlurHelper.CreateBlurredCopy(
                    image,
                    ScreenshotBlurPipeline.BlurIntensity,
                    ScreenshotBlurPipeline.BlurSaturation,
                    ScreenshotBlurPipeline.BlurBrightness);
            }

            lock (this.sync)
            {
                if (!this.screens.TryGetValue(screenInfo.Handle, out var state) ||
                    !ReferenceEquals(state, expectedState))
                {
                    blurredImage.Dispose();
                    blurredImage = null;
                    return;
                }

                state.Done?.Dispose();
                state.Done = blurredImage;
                blurredImage = null;
                state.DoneAt = this.TimeProvider.GetUtcNow();
                state.BlurInProgress = false;

                this.StartNextBlurIfQueued(screenInfo, state);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            blurredImage?.Dispose();
            lock (this.sync)
            {
                if (this.screens.TryGetValue(screenInfo.Handle, out var state) &&
                    ReferenceEquals(state, expectedState))
                {
                    state.BlurInProgress = false;
                    this.StartNextBlurIfQueued(screenInfo, state);
                }
            }

            ScreenshotBlurPipeline.CurrentTelemetry.WriteEvent(
                new { handle = screenInfo.Handle, error = ex.GetType().Name },
                "screenshotBlurFailed");
        }
        finally
        {
            // raised outside the lock, so a subscriber is free to call back into this pipeline
            // (e.g. TryGet) without risking a deadlock against the lock this method just held
            this.BlurCompleted?.Invoke(screenInfo);
        }
    }

    private void StartNextBlurIfQueued(ScreenInfo screenInfo, ScreenshotBlurState state)
    {
        if (state.Todo is not null)
        {
            var next = state.Todo;
            state.Todo = null;
            state.BlurInProgress = true;
            _ = Task.Run(() => this.RunBlur(screenInfo, state, next));
```

Apply every suggestion in this numbered group together; individual members are not complete fixes.

### Release the graphics HDC when setup fails

**Severity:** `medium`

GetHdc acquires the graphics device context before SetStretchBltMode can throw. Because the helper has not returned yet, the caller still holds HDC.Null and cannot release this acquisition in its finally block; release the local HDC and rethrow on that setup failure.

```suggestion
        try
        {
            _ = Gdi32.SetStretchBltMode(graphicsHdc, mode)
                .ThrowIfFailed();
            return graphicsHdc;
        }
        catch
        {
            graphics.ReleaseHdc(graphicsHdc);
            throw;
        }
```

### Keep the consumer alive under the write gate (2/5)

**Severity:** `high`

PauseWriting can currently return after the consumer releases the semaphore but before its Write finishes. Hold the gate through the action, release it in finally, and retain writer exceptions without terminating the consumer that later flush signals depend on.

```suggestion
                try
                {
                    action();
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    this.writerFailure ??= ex;
                }
                finally
                {
                    this.writeGate.Release();
                }
```

Apply every suggestion in this numbered group together; individual members are not complete fixes.

### Serialize flushes and surface write failure (3/5)

**Severity:** `high`

The channel marker only covers records preceding it; the consumer can already be writing a later record while Flush calls the writer on the calling thread. Use the same gate for Flush and report the retained write failure instead of implying that every queued record was persisted.

```suggestion

        this.writeGate.Wait();
        try
        {
            this.ThrowIfWriterFailed();
            this.writer.Flush();
        }
        finally
        {
            this.writeGate.Release();
        }
```

Apply every suggestion in this numbered group together; individual members are not complete fixes.

### Finish shutdown with explicit failure (4/5)

**Severity:** `high`

After draining the consumer, shutdown must also surface any retained Write failure. Serialize the final writer flush and add the shared failure check used by Flush, so a failed record cannot be silently treated as successful persistence.

```suggestion

        this.writeGate.Wait();
        try
        {
            this.writer.Flush();
            this.ThrowIfWriterFailed();
        }
        finally
        {
            this.writeGate.Release();
        }
    }

    private void ThrowIfWriterFailed()
    {
        if (this.writerFailure is not null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Telemetry writer failed while processing a record.", this.writerFailure);
        }
```

Apply every suggestion in this numbered group together; individual members are not complete fixes.

### Detach the context before disposing (5/5)

**Severity:** `high`

A writer Flush failure already makes Stop throw before clearing adapter, leaving a disposed adapter installed. Detach the field before disposal so a subsequent Flush is a no-op and Start can replace the failed writer; keep propagating the original shutdown failure.

```suggestion
        var adapter = this.adapter;
        this.adapter = null;
        adapter?.Dispose();
```

